## 13. Summary Scorecard

| Category | Score | Notes |
|----------|-------|-------|
| **Architecture** | 7/10 | Clean React+Zustand frontend. Backend needs decomposition. |
| **Feature Completeness** | 6/10 | Core modules work, statutory and notifications are scaffolded only |
| **Code Quality** | 6/10 | TypeScript types good, but giant files (Dashboard 60KB, CameraPunch 78KB) |
| **Security** | 8/10 | JWT, bcrypt, rate limiting, IP whitelisting, RBAC, audit logs — well above average |
| **Scalability** | 4/10 | SQLite dev mode, no caching, no queues, eager-loads everything |
| **Testing** | 2/10 | Only 3 test files, 1 e2e — critically low for a payroll system |
| **UI/UX** | 7/10 | Beautiful glassmorphism, but needs mobile optimization and role-specific dashboards |
| **Commercial Readiness** | 5/10 | Strong foundation but needs notifications, payments, compliance outputs to sell |
| **Documentation** | 3/10 | README exists but no API docs, architecture docs, or user guide |

> [!TIP]
> **Bottom Line**: SM Payroll has a **remarkably strong foundation** for an Indian SMB payroll product. The production/piece-rate integration, face-scan attendance, GPS geo-fencing, and salesman CRM are features that even ₹1000/emp/month competitors don't offer. With 3-6 months of focused work on compliance outputs, notifications, and testing, this can be a **₹10-50 Cr/year SaaS business** targeting India's 12M+ construction and 6.3M+ manufacturing firms.
